│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cry_lines_with_diag_paths                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed badly (0.45 vs prior 0.53). The fixed CNOT aggregators      │
│                          │ saturate entanglement before any training signal can shape the state, and CRX/CRY both    │
│                          │ mixing the same edges adds redundant parameters.                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I revert to the proven motif from the best prior ansatz (RY decoder → trainable CRY       │
│                          │ line-segment entanglers → center aggregation → center broadcast → RY/RX readout), which   │
│                          │ scored 0.53. The key improvements over that prior: (1) add a small trainable RX phase     │
│                          │ layer before the RY decoder so the circuit can shift the RX(±2π/3) feature into a more    │
│                          │ separable region before decoding (separating the three cell values requires both X and Y  │
│                          │ handling); (2) keep all entanglers parametrized (no fixed CNOTs) so the circuit starts    │
│                          │ near identity and trains smoothly under Adam. This keeps parameter count moderate and     │
│                          │ preserves the symmetric, tic-tac-toe-aware structure that generalized well (low           │
│                          │ generalization gap).                                                                      │
